<Base title="About Desks" description="Where Desks comes from, what is proven and what is not, related projects, and how to get help setting one up.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">Desks was extracted from one person's daily setup. This page says what is proven, what is not, and what else is out there.</p>

  <h2>What is proven, and what is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A year in the making, without knowing it.</strong> Desks grew out of the author's private working repository: first commit in October 2025, more than 1,200 commits since. The Desk pattern took its present shape there in September 2026 and ran in some twenty sessions of non-code work before being extracted.</li>
    <li><strong>The figures quoted in the docs</strong> (the share of usage taken by long sessions and by screenshots) are measurements from that one person's sessions. They are evidence, not a benchmark.</li>
    <li><strong>Tested on one setup only:</strong> Claude Code with a paid Claude plan, on a Mac. Nothing in the contract or the scripts depends on that, but no other assistant has been tried.</li>
    <li><strong>The scripts have unit tests</strong> that run on every change. They check what the scripts do. They do not show that a session goes well.</li>
    <li><strong>Newer parts are not yet tried in real use.</strong> Closing with your folder's own routine, and the rule that advice cannot finish unreviewed, were reviewed before they went in, and the script side has tests. Neither has been used in a real session yet. Each pull request says what was tried.</li>
    <li><strong>Not yet proven by other people.</strong> This version is early. If it does not help with your kind of work, that is a result worth reporting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related projects</h2>
  <p>Desks reuses what Claude Code already provides (skills, subagents, plugins, working copies) and does not rebuild it. Others have solved neighbouring problems, and for some needs they are the better choice:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/BeehiveInnovations/pal-mcp-server">PAL MCP Server</a>: a full multi-model toolkit for coding, with consensus across paid models.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/automazeio/ccpm">CCPM</a>: project management for software work, built on GitHub Issues and working copies.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/obra/superpowers">Superpowers</a>: a software-development method as a set of skills.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/ballred/obsidian-claude-pkm">obsidian-claude-pkm</a>: personal planning in an Obsidian vault.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What Desks adds is the combination: free review across vendors with a receipt, a short fixed list of things that wait for a person, and a focus on work that is not code.</p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>Good results from a modest plan. A Desk spends the expensive model on the conversation and the judgment, sends the reading to smaller models, and has answers checked by models that cost nothing. Fewer wrong answers reach you, and less compute is burned getting there. Review by other vendors reduces confident mistakes. It does not eliminate them.</p>
  <p>Desks is part of open-source and free culture, and proud of it. It exists because other people published their work for anyone to read, use and change. It is published the same way, so that whoever comes next can build on it without asking.</p>
  <p>Two kinds of disorder are welcome here. The one in how people think: write to a Desk the way thoughts actually arrive. And the one open source runs on: nobody is in charge of what you do with this. Fork it, bend it, port it.</p>
  <p>This is the version for Claude Code. The idea is not tied to it: if you use another assistant or app, you can use Desks to build your own version, <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>propose one in an issue</a>, or <a href="#work-with-me">hire me</a> to build it.</p>

  <h2>Contributing</h2>
  <p>The most useful contribution is a report of how a real session went, especially where it failed. Type <code>desk feedback</code> in any Desk: it drafts the report with the private parts removed and sends nothing unless you say so. Notes on driving a tool well (a browser, an app) and new review services are welcome. <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>How to contribute.</a> It is MIT licensed: fork it and make it yours.</p>

  <h2 id="work-with-me">Work with me</h2>
  <p>Desks is free and you can set it up yourself. This is what you can hire me for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fit it to how you already work.</strong> You have folders, habits and rules that came first. I set Desks up around them: where your own way of opening, reviewing and closing work exists, Desks uses it. Some habits may need a small change, and I will say which. You get a short written list of what changed and what did not. I did this on my own workspace; what it turned up is public in <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>the project's issues</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>One piece of work, with an end.</strong> Something gets in your way, or you need something Desks does not do yet. We agree what "done" looks like and a price. It is built in the open project, so the fix is yours and everyone's.</li>
    <li><strong>A conversation.</strong> An hour on how you work and whether Desks fits. If it does not, I will say so.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href={CONTACT}>Get in touch.</a></p>

  <p class="note">Desks is an independent project. It is not made by, endorsed by, or affiliated with Anthropic. Claude and Claude Code are Anthropic's names.</p>
</Base>
